<%
// ── What this document IS, and what it is NOT ───────────────────────────────
//
// **The OPERATOR’s sheet.** One row per line, the units spread across the seven
// `breakdown` state columns, both directions at once. It is the printable form
// of `/pick-sheet` — the same fold, the same rows, the same owner designation —
// so a picker can carry the screen to the shelves.
//
// 🔴 **It is NOT the packing list, and the split is the whole design.**
// `packing-list.eta` renders the SAME source collection for a different
// audience: it travels in the box, is served from a public route, names ONE
// direction and shows one Qty column. Custody buckets are internal — quoted,
// prepped, lost, damaged are facts about our warehouse, not about what a
// customer is receiving — so putting them on that document would have been the
// change this family exists to avoid making.
//
// ⚠️ **So the two bodies are near-neighbours and must not be merged.** Every
// difference below is a difference in AUDIENCE, and a `params` flag switching
// between them would put both audiences behind one golden that can only freeze
// one of them.
//
// ⚠️ **Every apostrophe in a `//` comment here is U+2019 (’), not ASCII, and
// that is a parser requirement rather than a typographic preference.** Eta’s
// lexer does not model a line comment, so a straight quote inside one opens a
// string as far as it is concerned. The full boundary — which quote shapes
// break, in which comment kind, and the two different error shapes they produce
// — is the CLAUDE.md section on it; this line exists only so the next editor of
// this file does not undo the punctuation.
const sheet = it.doc;

// `it.pickSheets`, NOT `it.orders`. A pick-sheets→packing_lists family resolves
// to {dates, money, icons, pickSheets} and gets no `it.orders` at all, so naming
// it here would throw at render AND fail the golden gate.
const u = it.pickSheets;

// EVERY date on this page goes through `it.dates.formatChicago*`, and the zone
// is named nowhere. date-fns resolves BOTH the parse and the format against the
// ambient zone unless each is given `{ in: … }`, stored business datetimes are
// Chicago-offset INSTANTS, and the render container sets no `TZ` — so an
// unpinned pair prints the UTC day and any boundary at or after 19:00 CDT is
// TOMORROW. `@cfs/core/utils/dates` owns that pinning; a call site that spells
// the zone itself is a second owner of it.

// ── One refusal, and it is the one that produces a WRONG document ───────────
//
// 🔴 **This document is PAGED, and a short one is indistinguishable from a small
// one once a template is running.**
//
// `PickSheet.orders` is *the orders on THIS PAGE*; `order_count`,
// `destination_count` and `quantity` describe the whole SCOPE. That split is
// right for a screen and is a trap for paper: a sheet rendered from a default
// page is silently short, showing a page of orders under a scope-wide total that
// disagrees with them. `GET /pick-sheets?address=` returns the complete sheet by
// construction; this assertion is what catches a caller that reached for a paged
// route instead.
//
// ⭐ **Asserted on `order_count`, NOT on `next_cursor`.** `next_cursor` is the
// assembler reporting on its own work, so a check against it can only ever agree
// with whatever the assembler believes; `orders.length === order_count` holds
// independently of it. Pair every fixed-point check with one that does not
// consult the same oracle.
if (sheet.orders.length !== sheet.order_count) {
  throw new Error(
    `pick-sheet: this document is SHORT — ${sheet.orders.length} order(s) on the page ` +
      `against ${sheet.order_count} in the scope. A PickSheet is paged by ORDER; build it ` +
      "from `GET /pick-sheets?address=`, which returns every order with `next_cursor: null` " +
      "by construction. Printing this page would put a scope-wide unit total over a subset " +
      "of the goods.",
  );
}

// ⭐ **There is deliberately NO leg refusal here, and that is the difference
// from `packing-list.eta` worth stating out loud.** That template throws on
// `leg === null`, because it accompanies ONE movement and because
// `groupByDestination` returns delivery as rental + sale and collection as
// rental only — two overlapping lists that double every rental line. This
// document calls neither builder and renders one row per line, so there is
// nothing to double; and a worker standing at a place wants both directions,
// which is exactly what the screen shows them.

// ── The columns ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 🔴 **The seven bucket keys and their headings come from the DECLARATION**,
// never from a literal list here. `BOOKING_BREAKDOWN_KEYS` and
// `BOOKING_BREAKDOWN_LABELS` are declared beside `BookingBreakdownSchema` in
// `@cfs/core`; the hand-written copy that used to sit in
// `manager/src/utils/fulfillmentStage.ts` had already drifted, saying "Out"
// where the declaration says **"Checked Out"**, and nothing asserted either
// string.
//
// ⚠️ `"booked"` is NOT one of them. It is DERIVED — reserved + prepped +
// out-unless-sale — so an eighth column for it would double-count three of the
// seven.
const bucketKeys = u.BOOKING_BREAKDOWN_KEYS;
const bucketLabels = u.BOOKING_BREAKDOWN_LABELS;

// ── The rows ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ⭐ **`dest.items` is rendered DIRECTLY — no `groupByDestination`, no
// `buildPackingList`.** Those two exist to select which lines a customer-facing
// list admits for one leg and to flatten them; both questions are the packing
// list’s. Here every line the fold put on the sheet is a row, in the fold’s own
// depth-first document order, with its `group` dividers still in place as
// banners. That is what makes this the screen on paper: the screen renders the
// same array.
//
// ⚠️ A `service` or `surcharge` line has no booking and therefore no bucket
// cells. It is still a row, because it is still on the order and the screen
// shows it — an internal sheet that silently dropped lines would be answering a
// different question than the one on screen.
const sections = sheet.orders.flatMap(function (order) {
  return order.destinations.map(function (dest) {
    // A leg’s rows, addressable by their own `path`. Built per leg and never
    // pooled: a `path` is a row identity WITHIN ONE DOCUMENT, so one map across
    // two orders would collide rows that are not the same row.
    const byPath = new Map();
    dest.items.forEach(function (entry) {
      byPath.set(entry.item.path.join(":"), entry.item);
    });
    return { order, dest, byPath };
  });
});

/**
 * What a NON-OWNER row says instead of a quantity.
 *
 * 🔴 **A row with no numbers and no explanation reads as missing data**, and on
 * this document there are always some: an aggregate booking has one owner
 * occurrence and every other occurrence renders blank. Measured on prod, 384
 * legs carry a booking standing for 2+ lines, so a picker meets this on ordinary
 * work — sees the same product twice, once with 56 and once with nothing, and
 * has no way to tell whether the blank one was forgotten.
 *
 * ⭐ It names the owner’s PARENT rather than the owner itself. The owner is the
 * same product by construction, so *"counted with Tent Tie Down"* on a Tent Tie
 * Down row says nothing; *"counted under Pop Up Tent (White)"* tells a picker
 * which kit to look in. `owner_path.at(-2)` is that parent, which is the same
 * value the manager’s row uses for the same sentence.
 *
 * ⚠️ Resolved by PATH, never by uid. `item.uid` repeats inside one document —
 * 18% of prod orders — so a uid lookup would find whichever occurrence came
 * first. A path is the row identity.
 */
function countedUnder(section, entry) {
  if (entry.owner_path === null) return "";
  const parent = section.byPath.get(entry.owner_path.slice(0, -1).join(":"));
  // No parent row means the owner hangs off the destination divider itself,
  // which is not in `items` — so there is nothing to name and "above" is the
  // honest answer. The rows are in document order, so it is also true.
  return parent ? `counted under ${parent.name}` : "counted above";
}

// 🔴 **Every number on a row comes from `pickSheetLineBooking`, and it returns
// null far more often than a reader expects.** A booking is aggregate per
// `(order, product, destination)`, so several lines in one leg legitimately name
// the same one — measured on prod at 384 legs where one booking stands for 2+
// lines. The fold designates exactly ONE owner occurrence
// (`PickSheetItem.owner_path`) and this helper answers only for it; every other
// occurrence renders blank cells rather than restating somebody else’s units.
//
// The naive lookup — `dest.bookings.find(b => b.uid === row.uid_booking)` —
// looks identical and is N× wrong. Do not write it.

// ── The facts this document states ──────────────────────────────────────────

// The direction, in the words a person uses. Three arms, not two: `null` is a
// real and ordinary state here, unlike on the packing list where it is refused.
const legLabel = sheet.leg === null
  ? "Both directions"
  : sheet.leg === "collection"
  ? "Collection"
  : "Delivery";

// ⚠️ The customer name is GUARDED, and null is an ANSWER rather than missing
// data. A destination-scoped sheet spans whatever is open at one place, and a
// shared stage legitimately holds two productions at once — measured on prod at
// `3100 W Fillmore St`, 17 orders across 12 organizations. Printing the first
// one in the letterhead would put one customer’s name on another’s goods.
//
// ⭐ Read off `sheet.organizations`, the distinct set across every page rather
// than off the page, so this cannot become "one customer" on a document that was
// assembled correctly but happens to begin with one.
const customerName = sheet.organizations.length === 1
  ? sheet.organizations[0].name
  : null;

// Both banner gates follow the same principle — a divider naming the only member
// of its level is noise — and each is computed from what its own level contains.
const showOrderBanner = sheet.orders.length > 1;
const showDestinationBanners = sections.length > 1;

const allRows = sections.flatMap(function (section) {
  return section.dest.items.map(function (entry) { return entry.item; });
});

// ⚠️ The Stock column is CONDITIONAL, for the reason the packing list and the
// receipt make their own columns conditional: a headed column carrying one value
// on every row reads as missing data rather than as inapplicable. `stock_method`
// earns its place only when the page distinguishes — a serialized line is one a
// picker checks an asset tag against, and a page of nothing but bulk lines holds
// no such row to find.
//
// ⭐ The seven bucket columns are NOT conditional, deliberately and against that
// same rule. They are the document’s SUBJECT: a page where every unit sits in
// `reserved` is telling a picker that nothing has been prepped yet, and a
// collapsing column set would make that page indistinguishable from one where
// the buckets were never asked about. The Stock column is a property of a row;
// these are the question.
const hasSerialized = allRows.some(function (row) { return row.stock_method === "serialized"; });

// Item Description, Type, [Stock], the seven buckets, Total.
const itemColumnCount = 2 + (hasSerialized ? 1 : 0) + bucketKeys.length + 1;

// The second alignment edge of the page: the #items column boundary NEAREST THE
// HALF-WAY POINT, rounding left. Same derivation as the quote, the invoice, the
// receipt and the packing list (see styles/base.css) — a fact about the shared
// page grid rather than about any one family.
const addressColumnCount = itemColumnCount - 1;
const addressBoundary = Math.floor((itemColumnCount - 1) / 2);

// The destinations table draws every leg on the sheet in one block, so its
// collection side is on when ANY leg has something coming back. `orderHasRentals`
// answers truthfully on a fulfillment-shaped line (`rental` is a
// `FULFILLMENT_LINE_ITEM_TYPES` member); the two money helpers in the shared
// sub-interface, `orderHasDiscount` and `orderHasTax`, would not — they select
// through `isPreTaxItem`, which is false for a line with no `price`, and a
// pick-sheet line carries a `FulfillmentItem`, which has none.
const hasCollection = sections.some(function (section) {
  return u.orderHasRentals(section.dest.items.map(function (entry) { return entry.item; }));
});
const deliverySpan = hasCollection ? addressBoundary : addressColumnCount;
const returnSpan = addressColumnCount - addressBoundary;

// ── What this template deliberately does NOT render ─────────────────────────
//
// • `PickSheetBooking.shortage` — units the allocator could not fill. Real, and
//   a different document’s job: a shortage is resolved by re-allocating or
//   substituting, which happens on the screen where those controls are. A number
//   on paper that nobody can act on from the dock is noise.
//
// • `PickSheetBooking.stores` — where the units are shelved. This is the field
//   most likely to be wanted next, and it is left out because a booking’s stores
//   are a LIST and this grid has no room for one; a Location column belongs to a
//   layout decision, not to a first version.
//
// • Any money. The fulfillment projection carries none by construction; the
//   fulfillment projection is defined as carrying no price and no financial
//   flags, and `it.money` is never called below.
//
// • A signature line. A hand-off signature is a real thing to want and it is a
//   decision about the WORKFLOW — who signs, for what, and whether an unsigned
//   sheet means anything — rather than a layout choice, so it is not invented
//   here. `packing-list.eta` and `receipt.eta` decline it for the same reason.
%>
<div id="grid-wrapper" style="--block-3-fraction: calc(<%= addressBoundary %> / <%= addressColumnCount %>)">
<%/* The letterhead takes a NAME, not an organization — a pick sheet carries a
     SET of `{uid, name}`, so no expression the partial could evaluate is right
     for every caller and each states how it got a name instead.

     `billing_address` is an explicit null: a pick sheet is a movement document
     and carries no billing address anywhere in its schema. Passing null says
     that, where omitting the prop would read as an oversight. */%>
<%~ await includeAsync("@partials/shared/letterhead.eta", {
     title: "Pick Sheet",
     customer_name: customerName,
     billing_address: null,
   }) %>

<p id="scope">
  <%/* `PickSheet.leg` is STAMPED on the document rather than re-derived at
       render. On the packing list that is what makes it a record; here it is
       what makes the sheet reproducible — a re-render after the goods went out
       must say what it was drawn for, not what custody has since become. */%>
  <strong><%= legLabel %></strong> —
  <%/* Three arms, one per `PickSheetScope.kind`. `order` is the DEGENERATE scope
       (api-cloudrun#821): the sheet is ONE order, so "everything at" reads as a
       place and "all open work for" claims a roll-up this document is not. */%>
  <%= sheet.scope.kind === "order"
    ? "for"
    : sheet.scope.kind === "organization"
    ? "all open work for"
    : "everything open at" %>
  <strong><%= sheet.scope.name %></strong>
  <%/* The gate IS printed here, unlike on the packing list, and the difference
       is the audience. `crew` and `counter` deliberately OVERLAP rather than
       partition — a leg we deliver and the customer returns is on both sheets —
       so a picker holding one of them needs to know which slice they have, or
       two sheets of the same place read as contradicting each other. On a
       customer-facing document it would be an internal dispatch concept with no
       meaning to the reader. */%>
  <% if (sheet.gate !== "all") { %>
  · <span class="gate"><%= sheet.gate === "crew" ? "Crew work" : "Will call / counter" %></span>
  <% } %>
</p>

<table id="details">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Printed:</th>
      <th scope="col">Order<%= sheet.order_count === 1 ? "" : "s" %>:</th>
      <th scope="col">Destinations:</th>
      <th scope="col">Total Units:</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <%/* `it.now` is `chicagoNowIso()` and sits on the same footing as every
           stored instant — unpinned it dated every quote rendered after 19:00
           Chicago as tomorrow. A pick sheet is printed at the moment picking
           starts, which is exactly when an evening boundary is crossed. */%>
      <td><%= it.dates.formatChicagoDateTime(it.now) %></td>
      <%/* The SCOPE counts, which by the refusal above are also the page counts.
           The NUMBER when there is exactly one order: `showOrderBanner` is
           `orders.length > 1`, so a single-order sheet emits no order banner and
           would otherwise name its order nowhere. */%>
      <td><%= sheet.order_count === 1 && sheet.orders[0]
        ? `#${sheet.orders[0].number}`
        : sheet.order_count %></td>
      <td><%= sheet.destination_count %></td>
      <td><%= sheet.quantity %></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<%/* ONE destinations table for the whole sheet, drawn by the shared partial.
     `dest.destination` IS a `DocDestination`, byte-identical to the one on its
     own order, so these pass straight through rather than being reshaped.

     ⚠️ A shared partial never NAMES a util namespace: `u` is handed in, and
     `u.getDestinationsLegend` / `u.isSameAsDeliveryDates` are the same function
     objects `it.orders` would have resolved to for an orders-source family. */%>
<%~ await includeAsync("@partials/shared/destinations.eta", {
     u,
     destinations: sections.map(function (section) { return section.dest.destination; }),
     addressColumnCount, hasCollection,
     deliverySpan, returnSpan,
   }) %>

<!-- Items -->
<section id="items">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col" class="wide">Item Description</th>
        <th scope="col">Type</th>
        <% if (hasSerialized) { %>
        <th scope="col">Stock</th>
        <% } %>
        <% bucketKeys.forEach(function (key) { %>
        <th scope="col" class="center bucket"><%= bucketLabels[key] %></th>
        <% }) %>
        <th scope="col" class="center">Total</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <%/* The banner spans this table exactly, from the same number the header is
           built from and the alignment edge above is derived from — a banner that
           disagreed with the header would take that edge with it. */%>
      <% const bannerSpan = itemColumnCount; %>
      <% let lastOrderUid; %>
      <% sections.forEach(function (section) { %>
        <% if (showOrderBanner && section.order.uid !== lastOrderUid) { %>
          <% lastOrderUid = section.order.uid; %>
        <%/* 🔴 The order banner is SAFETY, not decoration. A destination-scoped
             sheet spans whatever is open at one place, and a shared stage holds
             two productions at once — so a picker reading a mis-attributed row
             hands one production's gear to another. The organization rides the
             banner for the same reason, and only when the sheet actually spans
             customers (otherwise the letterhead already said it once). */%>
      <tr class="order-banner">
        <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan %>">
          <strong>Order #<%= section.order.number %></strong><%= section.order.subject ? ` — ${section.order.subject}` : "" %><% if (customerName === null && section.order.organization.name) { %> · <%= section.order.organization.name %><% } %>
        </td>
      </tr>
        <% } %>
        <% if (showDestinationBanners) { %>
      <tr class="destination-banner">
        <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan %>">
          <%/* `PickSheetDestination.name` is the destination DIVIDER's name —
               what the operator called this section on the order — and
               `quantity` is that leg's total, read off the document rather than
               summed from the rows. */%>
          <%= section.dest.name %> — <%= section.dest.quantity %> units
        </td>
      </tr>
        <% } %>
        <% section.dest.items.forEach(function (entry) { %>
          <% const item = entry.item; %>
          <%/* A `group` divider is a BANNER, not a row: it has no booking, no
               quantity and nothing to pick. Emitted in place rather than by
               grouping the rows first — the fold already returns them in
               depth-first document order, and re-grouping would be a second
               ordering authority that can disagree with the first. */%>
          <% if (item.type === "group") { %>
      <tr class="group-banner">
        <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan %>"><%= item.name %></td>
      </tr>
          <% } else { %>
          <%/* 🔴 `pickSheetLineBooking`, never a `find` on `uid_booking`. It
               answers only for the occurrence the fold designated as the
               booking's owner; every other occurrence of an aggregate booking
               gets null and renders blank cells, which is what stops one
               booking's units being stated N times. */%>
          <% const booking = u.pickSheetLineBooking(section.dest, entry); %>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <%= item.name %><%/* The blank-cells explanation, on the row that is
               blank rather than in a legend at the foot of the page: a picker
               reads one row at a time and a legend is on the wrong page by the
               time it is needed. */%><% if (entry.owner_path !== null) { %><span class="counted-under"> · <%= countedUnder(section, entry) %></span><% } %>
        </td>
        <td class="capitalize"><%= item.type %></td>
        <% if (hasSerialized) { %>
        <td><%= item.stock_method === "serialized" ? "Serialized" : "" %></td>
        <% } %>
        <% bucketKeys.forEach(function (key) { %>
        <%/* An empty cell rather than a `0`, and the difference is legibility at
             arm's length: measured across every sampled booking, exactly ONE of
             the seven buckets is non-zero on a given row, so printing the zeros
             would put six noise digits beside every real one. */%>
        <td class="center"><%= booking && booking.breakdown[key] ? booking.breakdown[key] : "" %></td>
        <% }) %>
        <%/* The row total is the BOOKING's `quantity`, not the sum of the cells
             beside it — a second reading of the same fact, deliberately. The
             breakdown is a current-state partition, so the two agree by
             construction; where they do not, the stored booking is wrong and this
             sheet is the surface that shows it. */%>
        <td class="center"><%= booking ? booking.quantity : "" %></td>
      </tr>
          <% } %>
        <% }) %>
      <% }) %>
      <%/* The document total, read off the sheet rather than summed from the
           rows above. `PickSheet.quantity` is the units across every admitted
           leg, computed once in the fold; re-deriving it here would be a second
           authority, and the obvious per-line derivation through `uid_booking`
           is the one that is N× wrong. */%>
      <tr class="grand-total">
        <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan - 1 %>"><strong>Total Units</strong></td>
        <td class="center"><strong><%= sheet.quantity %></strong></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<% if (sheet.missing_order_uids.length > 0 || sheet.notice) { %>
<%/* 🔴 SURFACED, never dropped. `missing_order_uids` names orders the scope
     admits whose fulfillment document could not be read — `bookings` and the
     projection disagree, which is a finding
     (`api-cloudrun/scripts/audit-fulfillment-projection.ts`) rather than a
     display problem. The person holding this paper is the only one positioned to
     notice that the goods in front of them are short.

     `notice` is set only when the page was clipped, and by the refusal above a
     clipped page never reaches here — it is printed anyway because a notice the
     schema can set and the document never shows is a silent channel. */%>
<section id="exceptions">
  <% if (sheet.missing_order_uids.length > 0) { %>
  <p>
    <strong>Incomplete:</strong>
    <%= sheet.missing_order_uids.length %>
    order<%= sheet.missing_order_uids.length === 1 ? "" : "s" %>
    in this scope could not be read and <%= sheet.missing_order_uids.length === 1 ? "is" : "are" %>
    NOT listed above. Do not treat this sheet as the complete picture until that is resolved.
  </p>
  <% } %>
  <% if (sheet.notice) { %>
  <p><strong>Notice:</strong> <%= sheet.notice %></p>
  <% } %>
</section>
<% } %>
</div>
